---
// "More writing" — the two most recent other pieces, at the foot of an
// article. specs/pages/writing-article.md §2.3.
//
// Not "related": a similarity list computed from shared tags is a taxonomy
// pretending to be relevance. The caller decides what the two are and whether
// there are enough of them to show any (the threshold is two others); this
// component decides only how they look. On /he/writing/[id]/ the set is
// translations only, which is that template's argument, not this one's.
//
// Two most recent, never more: past that the end matter stops being end
// matter and becomes a second index.
//
// ── the Hebrew heading ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Hebrew is unicameral, so the uppercase-plus-tracking label treatment has
// nothing to act on and letter-spacing on a Hebrew run is a defect rather
// than a style (specs/design/typography.md §6, specs/navigation.md N-7).
//
// The two treatments are written as exclusive branches, not as a base with a
// Hebrew override. The stylesheet could do base-plus-override because
// `.siblings-title-he` was written second and won a source-order tie at equal
// specificity; utilities all land in one layer where Tailwind's own sort
// decides, and an element carrying both `uppercase` and `normal-case` would
// resolve by a rule this file does not control.
//
// `tracking-[normal]` and never `tracking-normal`: the latter resolves to
// `0em`, a computed zero. What the specs pin on a Hebrew run is the KEYWORD
// `normal`, which is the font's own spacing and not an override of it.
//
// ── the list ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// The stylesheet spaced these with `li + li`. Here the gap is put on every
// item after the first by index instead: same set of elements, and it avoids
// an arbitrary variant, which Tailwind sorts after everything else and which
// would therefore be the one rule in the list whose precedence is not obvious
// from reading it.
//
// The links drop the global underline at rest and take it back on hover,
// which is why `decoration-current` and `decoration-auto` are spelled out
// beside `underline`: the declaration this replaced was the `text-decoration`
// SHORTHAND, and its reset half is load-bearing. It also put decoration
// colour back to currentcolor and thickness back to auto over the base `a`
// rule's --border-strong at 1px. The `underline` utility is a longhand and
// sets the line alone. Dropping the pair costs a hairline of underline
// weight — exactly the class of change that ships unnoticed.

interface Props {
  /** The section's heading. */
  heading: string;
  /** The page's locale, which decides the heading treatment. */
  lang: 'en' | 'he';
  /** Already sliced to the two most recent by the caller. */
  items: { href: string; title: string }[];
}

const { heading, lang, items } = Astro.props;
---

<section class="mbs-10">
  <h2
    class:list={[
      'font-mono text-size-label text-muted',
      lang === 'he' ? 'normal-case tracking-[normal]' : 'uppercase tracking-label',
    ]}
  >{heading}</h2>
  <ul class="list-none m-0 p-0 mbs-3">
    {
      items.map((item, index) => (
        <li class:list={[index > 0 && 'mbs-3']}>
          <a
            class="text-size-body text-text-strong no-underline [&:hover]:underline [&:hover]:decoration-current [&:hover]:decoration-auto [&:hover]:underline-offset-[0.2em]"
            href={item.href}
          >{item.title}</a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
</section>
